// Pins the C that an integer `match` lowers to: a `switch` whose every arm body
// is its OWN BRACED BLOCK.
//
// No golden program contained an integer match at all before this one, so the
// lowering was unpinned. It mattered: an arm body is a STMT_BLOCK that emits no
// braces of its own, so an arm's declarations landed directly in the switch's
// single compound statement - a declaration after a label, in scope but
// UNINITIALISED in every sibling arm. A `__attribute__((cleanup))` on one is a hard
// clang error, which is what broke the first attempt at releasing container locals
// at scope exit (#466), and any release emitted for it would have freed a garbage
// pointer in the arms that never ran its initialiser.
//
// Bracing is behaviour-neutral, which is exactly why it needs a GOLDEN rather than
// a behavioural test: removing the braces again would not change any program's
// output (clang accepts the declaration as an extension), so only a snapshot of the
// emitted C can catch the regression. Mutation-verified by removing the braces and
// watching this snapshot diff.

fn pick(n: int) -> int {
    match n {
        0 => {
            var a: [int] = []
            a.push(3)
            a.push(4)
            return a[0] + a[1]
        }
        1 => { return 1 }
        _ => {
            var z: [int] = []
            z.push(-1)
            return z[0]
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    println(pick(0))
    println(pick(1))
    println(pick(9))
}
